98	\begin{abstract}
99	Starting from a single abstract category and three operation types, an induction procedure derives twelve invariants --- the primitives of the Universal Imscriptive Grammar --- as the minimal complete independent set of the
100	construction. No primitive is stipulated; each emerges uniquely from the operations' own structure. Incorporating
101	Graham Priest's Logic of Paradox yields \text{{\igfont 𐑣}} as the categorical Inclosure Schema, and makes the crystal
102	size $3^3 \times 4^5 \times 5^4 = 17{,}280{,}000$ derivable from the logical structure of the induction alone. Cantor's
103	diagonal and G\"odel's incompleteness sentence are the same Inclosure construction at successive overflow levels; the
104	grammar occupies Level~2, co-typed with the Millennium Problems. This document is the $\delta$ half of a Frobenius pair
105	with its companion (\emph{So Below}); $\mu \circ \delta = \text{id}$: the grammar applied to its own derivation returns
106	itself. All eight open questions from the original manuscript are resolved in \S VIII.
107	\end{abstract}
