113	\paragraph*{Terminological note: \emph{imscriptive}.}
114	Throughout this document the adjective \emph{imscriptive} is used in preference to
115	\emph{holographic} to describe the
116	bulk-boundary imscription property realised by \text{{\igfont 𐑦}} and \text{{\igfont 𐑸}}. A system
117	\emph{imscriptive} if its boundary surface fully imscribes all interior degrees of freedom --- so that the bulk is
118	recoverable from boundary data alone, without remainder. The verb is \emph{to imscribe}; the noun is
119	\emph{imscription}.
120	The term is constructed from \emph{im-} (Latin, within/immersed; cf.\ \emph{immerse},
121	\emph{implant}, \emph{imbue}) and
122	\emph{-scriptive} (from L.\ \emph{scribere}, to write): to imscribe is to write the interior into
