138	
139	\subsubsection{The Abstract Category}
140	The starting object is an \textbf{abstract category} $\mathcal{C}$ in the sense of Eilenberg and Mac Lane (1945). A
141	
142	\begin{itemize}
143	 \item A collection of \emph{objects} $\text{ob}(\mathcal{C})$
144	 \item A collection of \emph{morphisms} $\text{hom}(A, B)$ for each pair of objects $A, B$
145	 \item A \emph{composition law} $\circ: \text{hom}(B,C) \times \text{hom}(A,B) \to
146	 \text{hom}(A,C)$
147	 \item \emph{Identity morphisms} $\text{id}_A \in \text{hom}(A,A)$ satisfying $f \circ
148	 \text{id}_A = f = \text{id}_B
149	\circ f$
150	 \item Associativity: $(h \circ g) \circ f = h \circ (g \circ f)$
151	\end{itemize}
152	
153	This is the minimal non-trivial mathematical object that captures both \emph{structure} (objects)
154	\emph{transformation} (morphisms) without presupposing elements, order, or metric. It is strictly weaker than set
155	theory --- a category need not have sets as objects --- and strictly more structured than a bare graph, because
