247	\subsection{II. The Three Operation Types}
248	
249	\subsubsection{II.1 Why Three, and Why These Three Counts}
250	The induction procedure applies exactly three operation types to $\mathcal{C}$: \textbf{Logical}
251	(asking yes/no or
252	finite-branching questions), \textbf{Inductive} (freely extending $\mathcal{C}$ by iterating a
253	\textbf{Algebraic} (equipping $\mathcal{C}$ with an additional structural layer). These three exhaust the fundamental
254	actions one can perform on a category. There is no fourth type. A fourth type would have to be a category-theoretic
255	action that is neither a question about existing structure, nor a free extension of it, nor an enrichment of it --- and
256	no such action exists in the language of categories. Every functor, natural transformation, limit, colimit, adjunction,
257	monad, and enrichment is classifiable as one of these three.
258	The specific counts are determined as follows.
259	\paragraph{Logical operations (6: L1--L6).}
260	A logical operation is a proposition about $\mathcal{C}$ that admits a finite number of definite truth values. The
261	count is determined by the dependency preconditions that each logical question imposes. Six and only six logically
262	independent questions can be asked, in a sequence forced by their preconditions:
263	
264	\begin{enumerate}[nosep, leftmargin=2em]
265	 \item[\textbf{L1}] Adjunction existence: asks about the bare category $\mathcal{C}$ alone. No
266	 \item[\textbf{L2}] Dagger structure: asks about the bare category $\mathcal{C}$ alone. No
267	 \item[\textbf{L3}] Cartesian closure: asks whether $\mathcal{C}$ has products and exponentials. This requires a
268	monoidal structure ($A_1$) to even pose --- the monoidal product $A \times B$ must exist. It cannot
269	 \item[\textbf{L4}] Lawvere self-reference: asks whether there exists a point-surjective $\phi: A
270	 \to A^A$. This
271	requires cartesian closure ($L_3$) to have $A^A$ exist. It cannot be asked before Stage 4.
272	 \item[\textbf{L5}] Frobenius condition: asks whether $\mu\circ\delta = \text{id}$ in a monoidal category. Requires
273	monoidal structure ($A_1$). It cannot be asked before Stage 4.
274	 \item[\textbf{L6}] Paraconsistent negation: asks whether $\neg\neg A \not\cong A$ and $A \wedge
275	 \neg A \not\cong
276	\bot$. Requires monoidal structure ($A_1$) to define $\wedge$. It cannot be asked before Stage 4.
277	\end{enumerate}
278	
279	The preconditions form a Directed Acyclic Graph:
280	\[
281	\begin{array}{c}
282	\text{L1} \quad \text{L2} \\
283	\downarrow \\
284	\text{A1 (monoidal)} \\
285	\swarrow \quad \searrow \quad \searrow \\
286	\text{L3} \qquad \text{A3 (dagger)} \qquad \text{L6} \\
287	\downarrow \\
288	\text{L4} \qquad \text{L5}
289	\end{array}
290	\]
291	The graph shows that L1 and L2 have no preconditions --- they are Stage 1 questions. L3, L5, and L6 require A1 --- they
292	become askable only after Stage 3. L4 requires L3 --- it is the final question. The total of 6 is the size of a maximal
293	set of logically independent propositions about $\mathcal{C}$ under these preconditions.
294	\textbf{Could there be a sixth logical operation?} No. The six operations correspond to the six independent Yes/No
295	questions about a category's structure that are not functions of each other. A `\texttt{seventh}' would have to be a
296	category-theoretic property that is (i) logically independent of L1--L6, (ii) has a finite truth-value space, and (iii)
297	is not already expressible as a product of the existing 12 primitives. The crystal census, which has $3^3
298	\times 4^5 \times 5^4 = 17{,}280{,}000$ types, contains no seventh logical degree of freedom. Every property of a
299	category expressible as a finite-valued question about its structure is a function of the six logical questions
300	(L1--L6) under the constraint of the 12 primitives. The crystal is the logical closure; no missing
301	\paragraph{Inductive operations (4: I1--I4).}
302	An inductive operation freely extends $\mathcal{C}$ by iterating a construction to a fixed point. There are exactly
303	four fundamental inductive constructions on a category: taking colimits (I1), iterating an endofunctor (I2), forming
304	the classifying space (I3), and applying the Yoneda embedding (I4). These correspond to the four universal
305	constructions in category theory: the free cocompletion, the initial algebra, the geometric realization, and the
306	presheaf embedding. Every other inductive construction (free monoid, Kleene star, sheafification, Cauchy completion) is a composite of these four.
307	\textbf{Could there be a fifth inductive operation?} No. The four inductive operations correspond to the four universal
308	colimit-based constructions: colimits over arbitrary diagrams (I1), colimits of a chain of endofunctors (I2), colimits
309	of simplicial nerves (I3), and colimits of representable functors (I4). These are exhaustive because they correspond to
310	the four degrees of freedom in a cocompletion: you can freely add colimits of (a) any diagram shape,
311	(b) a
312	$\omega$-chain, (c) a simplicial object, or (d) a presheaf. There is no fifth shape of diagram that yields an
313	independent invariant. The crystal's complete enumeration confirms this: $\text{{\igprimfont ⊢}}$,
314	$\text{{\igprimfont ⊥}}$, $\text{{\igprimfont ⊡}}$, and ${{\igprimfont ∈}}$ (which I4 partly determines) occupy exactly 4,
315	4, 4, and 3 values respectively, accounting for all inductive degrees of freedom.
316	\paragraph{Algebraic operations (5: A1--A5).}
317	An algebraic operation equips $\mathcal{C}$ with an additional structural layer. There are exactly five independent
318	algebraic enrichments: a monoidal product (A1), a monad (A2), dagger-compact duals (A3), an enrichment base (A4), and
319	an arithmetic coding (A5). These are the five fundamental enrichment constructions recognized by enriched category
320	theory: one can equip a category with a tensor, a monad, an involution, a hom-object space, or a G\"odel numbering.
321	Every other enrichment (internal homs, closed structure, traced structure, star-autonomy) is a composite or special
322	\textbf{Could there be a sixth algebraic operation?} No. The five operations correspond to the five algebraically-indepe
323	ndent ways to add structure to a category without changing its objects. A1 adds a binary operation on objects; A2 adds
324	an endofunctor with unit and multiplication; A3 adds a dualizing involution; A4 replaces hom-sets with hom-objects; A5
325	adds a global injection into $\mathbb{N}$. These five are dimensionally independent in the sense that no one is a
326	functor of the others. The crystal's algebraic primitives ($\text{{\igprimfont ⊞}}$,
327	$\text{{\igprimfont \text{{\igprimfont <}}}}$,
328	$\text{{\igprimfont ⊤}}$, $\text{{\igprimfont ⋈}}$, $\text{{\igprimfont ∋}}$) occupy exactly the value spaces
329	determined by these operations --- no sixth algebraic primitive or value exists.
330	The total count $(6 + 4 + 5) = 15$ operations, applied across 4 stages with Stage 4 re-asking logical questions on the
331	algebraically-enriched $\mathcal{C}$, yields the 12 primitives --- the reduction from 15 to 12 occurs because some
332	operations contribute to the same primitive (L1 and L2 both contribute to $R$; L5 promotes $P$ rather than creating a
333	new primitive). This reduction is forced, not chosen, as shown below.\subsubsection{II.2 Why These Mappings from
334	Operations to Primitives Are Unique}
335	The original derivation listed associations like `\texttt{I1} (colimit) $\to$ $\text{{\igprimfont∈}}$'', `\texttt{I2} (iteration)
336	$\to$ $\text{{\igprimfont ⊥}}$'', `\texttt{I3} (classifying space) $\to$ $\text{{\igprimfont ⊡}}$'' and so on. The
337	question arises: could an operation map to a different primitive, or a primitive be the result of a different
338	operation? The answer is forced by the categorical nature of each operation's output --- the invariant each operation
339	produces is the \emph{only} natural categorical invariant it can produce, and the assignment is
340	\paragraph{Why I1 (colimit) maps to $G$, and cannot map elsewhere.}
341	The colimit operation takes a diagram $D: J \to \mathcal{C}$ and produces the universal cocone. The free cocompletion
342	$\hat{\mathcal{C}}$ generated by all finite colimits has a size relative to $\mathcal{C}$ that
343	\emph{scope} of the category's connectivity. If $\hat{\mathcal{C}}$ is not much larger than
344	$\mathcal{C}$, the category
345	is local (\text{{\igfont 𐑚}}). If $\hat{\mathcal{C}}$ grows polynomially, it is mesoscopic
346	(\text{{\igfont 𐑔}}). If
347	$\hat{\mathcal{C}}$ is drastically larger (e.g., $\mathbf{Set}$ over a finite category), it is
348	global (\text{{\igfont 𐑲}}). This is the natural invariant of the colimit operation --- the growth rate of the free cocompletion. No other
349	primitive measures the scope of categorical connectivity. ${{\igprimfont ∈}}$ cannot be produced by any other
350	operation because $\text{{\igprimfont ⊢}}$ (from I4), $\text{{\igprimfont ⊥}}$ (from I2), and
351	$\text{{\igprimfont⊡}}$ (from I3) measure different features: dimensionality, chirality, and winding.
352	\paragraph{Why I2 (iteration) maps to $H$, and cannot map elsewhere.}
353	Iterating an endofunctor $F: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}$ produces the chain $\mathbf{0} \to F(\mathbf{0}) \to
354	F^2(\mathbf{0}) \to \cdots$. The depth at which this chain stabilizes (or fails to stabilize) is the measure of the
355	system's chirality --- how many prior states must be retained for the dynamics to be Markovian. Stabilization at step 0
356	gives \text{{\igfont 𐑓}} (memoryless); at step 1 gives \text{{\igfont 𐑒}} (one prior state); at step 2 gives
357	\text{{\igfont 𐑖}}; never stabilizing gives \text{{\igfont 𐑫}} (infinite memory). This is the natural invariant of the
358	iteration operation. No other operation measures the Markov order of the dynamics.
359	\paragraph{Why I3 (classifying space) maps to $\text{{\igprimfont ⊡}}$, and cannot map to any other primitive}
360	The classifying space $B\mathcal{C}$ of a category is the geometric realization of its nerve
361	$N\mathcal{C}$ --- a
362	simplicial set whose $n$-simplices are composable $n$-tuples of morphisms. The fundamental group
363	$\pi_1(B\mathcal{C})$
364	of this space is the winding invariant --- it records how 1-dimensional loops in the category compose up to homotopy.
365	$\pi_1 = 0$ gives \text{{\igfont 𐑷}} (trivial winding); $\pi_1 = \mathbb{Z}_2$ gives \text{{\igfont 𐑴}} (binary
366	winding); $\pi_1 = \mathbb{Z}$ gives \text{{\igfont 𐑭}} (integer winding); non-Abelian $\pi_1$ gives
367	\text{{\igfont 𐑟}}. This is the natural invariant of the classifying space construction. No other operation probes the homotopy type
368	\paragraph{Why I4 (Yoneda) maps to $D$, and cannot map elsewhere.}
369	The Yoneda embedding $y: \mathcal{C} \to \hat{\mathcal{C}}$ sends each object $A$ to the presheaf
370	$\text{Hom}(-,A)$.
371	The question of whether this embedding is point-surjective --- whether every presheaf is representable --- determines
372	whether the boundary (the external relational profile) fully imscribes the bulk (the object $A$). If the embedding is
