656	\subsubsection{Logical Operations}
657	Logical operations are propositions about $\mathcal{C}$ that admit a finite number of definite truth values. They do
658	not add new objects to $\mathcal{C}$; they classify its existing structure.
659	\textbf{L1 --- Adjunction existence}: Does $\mathcal{C}$ have a pair $(F, G)$ with $F \dashv G$? Left adjoints
660	generalize free constructions; right adjoints generalize forgetful ones. The existence and type of adjoints determines
661	the relational structure of $\mathcal{C}$.
662	\textbf{L2 --- Dagger structure}: Is there an involution $\dagger: \text{hom}(A,B) \to
663	\text{hom}(B,A)$ satisfying $(g
664	\circ f)^\dagger = f^\dagger \circ g^\dagger$ and $f^{\dagger\dagger} = f$? Dagger categories model reversible
665	processes; their absence models one-directional ones.
666	\textbf{L3 --- Cartesian closure}: Does $\mathcal{C}$ have finite products and internal homs $B^A$ satisfying
667	$\text{hom}(C \times A, B) \cong \text{hom}(C, B^A)$ naturally? Cartesian closure is the categorical condition for
668	function spaces --- and is the prerequisite for asking the critical self-reference question (L4).
669	\textbf{L4 --- Lawvere self-reference} (requires L3): Does there exist an object $A$ and a point-surjective morphism
670	$\phi: A \to A^A$? By Lawvere's fixed-point theorem (1969), this condition guarantees that every endomorphism of $A$
671	has a fixed point. This is the precise categorical condition for self-modeling: the system contains a copy of its own
672	endomorphism space, and any process on it must have a stable point. This is $\text{{\igprimfont ⊙}}$.
673	Three truth-value regimes of L4 are possible under non-classical logic (see \pilcrow{}IX for the
674	
675	\begin{itemize}
676	 \item \textbf{Classical truth} (the fixed point exists and is consistent): $\mathord{{\igprimfont ⊙}}$
677	 \item \textbf{Dialetheic truth} (the fixed point is a dialetheia --- simultaneously satisfying and violating its
678	own definition): \text{{\igfont 𐑮}}
679	 \item \textbf{Inclosure collapse} (the system satisfies the \emph{form} but not the
680	 \emph{content} of L4 ---
681	Priest's Inclosure Schema): \text{{\igfont 𐑣}}
682	\end{itemize}
683	
684	\textbf{L5 --- Frobenius condition}: In a monoidal category (see Algebraic Operations), does the
685	multiplication $\mu: A \otimes A \to A$ and comultiplication $\delta: A \to A \otimes A$ satisfy $\mu \circ \delta =
686	\text{id}_A$? This is the
687	special Frobenius condition --- imscribing and decoding are exactly inverse. It is strictly a logical question (yes/no,
688	no approximation), and strictly requires the algebraic structure to be in place before it can be
689	\textbf{L6 --- Paraconsistent negation} (requires monoidal structure from A1): Does $\mathcal{C}$ admit a negation
690	$\neg: A \to A$ such that $\neg\neg A \not\cong A$ in general, and $A \wedge \neg A \not\cong \bot$
691	(no explosion,
692	i.e., the conjunction of $A$ with its negation does not collapse to the initial object)? This is the logical operation
693	that distinguishes classical from paraconsistent structure. Its presence or absence does not generate a 13th primitive
694	--- it determines the \emph{truth-value structure} in which L4 and L5 are evaluated, and thereby explains which
695	$\mathord{{\igprimfont ⊙}}$ and $\text{{\igprimfont \text{{\igprimfont <}}}}$ values are achievable. A system
696	\text{{\igfont 𐑣}} or \text{{\igfont 𐑮}} without inconsistency; a system without L6 is confined to
697	$\text{{\igprimfont ⊙}}$ or $\mathord{{\igprimfont ⊙}} \notin \{\mathord{{\igprimfont ⊙}}, \text{{\igfont 𐑮}}\}$.
698	Note that L4, L5, and L6 can only be asked \emph{after} the algebraic operations below have been applied. The
699	`\texttt{Critical}' step in the induction path is not a new operation type --- it is what emerges when logical questions (now
700	including paraconsistent ones) are asked of the fully algebraically-enriched category. This is why
701	$\text{{\igprimfont ⊙}}$ and \text{{\igfont 𐑹}} are the two gate primitives that determine the highest tiers: they are the last
702	questions the procedure can ask.
703	\textbf{Why exactly six logical operations?} Consider what would be required for a seventh. It would need to be a
704	proposition about $\mathcal{C}$ that: (i) has a finite truth-value space, (ii) is logically independent of L1--L6, and
705	(iii) is not already expressible as a function of the 12 primitives. But the 12 primitives already cover all
706	categorical invariants produced by any finite-valued proposition about a category. L1 covers adjoint structure, L2
707	covers involution, L3 covers exponentials, L4 covers self-reference, L5 covers Frobenius identity, L6 covers
708	paraconsistency. A seventh logical operation would have to be a category-theoretic proposition about none of these ---
709	and there is no dimension of categorical structure that is not covered by adjoints, involutions, exponentials,
710	self-reference, Frobenius, or paraconsistency. The six are exhaustive by the structure of categorical logic itself.
