717	\subsubsection{Inductive Operations}
718	Inductive operations build new categorical structures from $\mathcal{C}$ by iterating a construction
719	\textbf{I1 --- Colimit (free construction)}: Given a diagram $D: J \to \mathcal{C}$, its colimit
720	$\text{colim}\, D$ is
721	the universal cocone. Taking colimits of all finite diagrams generates the free cocompletion
722	$\hat{\mathcal{C}}$. The
723	rate of growth of $\hat{\mathcal{C}}$ with respect to $\mathcal{C}$ measures the scope of the category --- local,
724	\textbf{I2 --- Iteration of an endofunctor}: Given $F: \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{C}$, form the chain
725	$\mathbf{0} \to
726	F(\mathbf{0}) \to F^2(\mathbf{0}) \to \cdots$. The colimit of this chain is the initial $F$-algebra. The depth at which
727	the chain stabilizes (if it does) is a measure of the system's chirality. Non-stabilization
728	\text{{\igfont 𐑫}}; stabilization at step $n$ to $\text{{\igprimfont ⊥}}_n$.
729	\textbf{I3 --- Classifying space construction}: Given $\mathcal{C}$, form its nerve $N\mathcal{C}$
730	(the simplicial set
731	of composable morphism chains) and its geometric realization $B\mathcal{C} = |N\mathcal{C}|$. The homotopy type of
732	$B\mathcal{C}$ --- specifically its fundamental group $\pi_1(B\mathcal{C})$ --- is the winding invariant. $\pi_1 = 0$
733	gives \text{{\igfont 𐑷}}; $\pi_1 = \mathbb{Z}_2$ gives \text{{\igfont 𐑴}}; $\pi_1 = \mathbb{Z}$
734	gives \text{{\igfont 𐑭}}; non-Abelian $\pi_1$ gives \text{{\igfont 𐑟}}.
735	\textbf{I4 --- Yoneda embedding}: The functor $y: \mathcal{C} \to \hat{\mathcal{C}}$ defined by
736	$y(A) =
737	\text{Hom}(-,A)$ is fully faithful --- it embeds $\mathcal{C}$ into its presheaf category. The Yoneda lemma states that
738	this embedding is identity-preserving: $A$ is fully determined by its morphisms, i.e., by its relational boundary. This
739	is the categorical form of \text{{\igfont 𐑦}}: boundary (the presheaf $h_A$) imscribes bulk (the object $A$). Every
740	object in $\mathcal{C}$ is imscriptively reconstructible from its external relational profile.
741	\textbf{Why exactly four inductive operations?} The four operations correspond to the four universal colimit-based
742	constructions that produce an independent categorical invariant. I1 (freely adding all colimits) measures the scope of
743	connectivity via the growth rate of the cocompletion. I2 (freely adding an initial algebra) measures chirality via the
744	stabilization ordinal. I3 (freely adding geometric realization) measures winding via the homotopy group. I4 (freely
745	adding representable presheaves) measures dimensionality via the surjectivity of the Yoneda embedding. Any other
746	inductive construction --- free monoid, Kleene star, Cauchy completion, sheafification --- is a composition of these
747	four base constructions: for instance, the free monoid on an object is a special case of I2 (the list monad is the
748	initial algebra of $F(X) = 1 + A \otimes X$), and sheafification is a composite of I1, I4, and a localization (a
749	logical operation, not a new inductive one). There is no inductive construction that produces an invariant not already
750	captured by $\text{{\igprimfont ⊢}}$, $\text{{\igprimfont ⊥}}$, $\text{{\igprimfont ⊡}}$, and
751	$\text{{\igprimfont∈}}$.\begin{center}
752	\rule{0.5\textwidth}{0.4pt}
753	\end{center}
